Sub-pixel stabilisation while walking, too.

Walking moves surfaces at different depths differently, so the whole-picture
homography from 0007 does not apply, and fitted anyway it biased every path.
It is now used only for a turn (corners shift sideways by about the same
amount: median shift >= 4 px with spread <= 35% of it). Otherwise each face
fits a homography over the faces within two steps of it (one step when the
larger patch crosses a surface edge); a patch is trusted only within the
rounding (every corner <= 1.6 px, RMS <= 0.5 px). Corners take the mean of
their predictions and keep one shared value. Every corner starts where its
path ended even when this picture is not smoothed, and a corner without a
vote keeps half of last frame's offset, so corners near surface edges ease
back instead of jumping. A corner -> faces index keeps the patches cheap.

Recorded walk: relative corner kicks 0.93/0.79/0.50 -> 0.42/0.27/0.17 px
median (small/mid/large faces), p99 no worse than the game's. Recorded turn
as in 0.1.8 or better. No measurable frame-time cost against 0.1.8.
tests/geometry_interp.c: walk_stabilisation (no bias, shared corners).

